**Workload:** script output (static report), but the page runs all day on a monitor

#### [Security] Unescaped values go into `innerHTML`
- **Where:** the `rows += ...` string and `$('#jobs').html(rows)`
- **Issue:** `j.name`, `j.status` and `j.finished` are put into HTML as raw text. A job or file name with `<` in it breaks the page or runs script. Also, make sure the CLI escapes `</script>` inside `{{JOBS_JSON}}`.
- **Fix:** build the cells with `textContent`, as in the code below.

#### [Bug] "Updated" shows the wrong time
- **Where:** last `setInterval`
- **Issue:** it prints the current clock of the monitor, not the time of the backup run. The page is static, so after two days it still says "Updated 10:41". People will think the data is fresh when it is not.
- **Fix:** let the CLI write the generation time into the page (`{{GENERATED_AT}}`).

#### [High] Three JavaScript animation loops that run all day
- **Where:** `spin()` with `requestAnimationFrame`, the blinking `setInterval`, the `fadeOut/fadeIn` every second
- **Issue:** the page never changes after it is written, but the browser must run script and update styles all day, forever. The spinner is also misleading, because nothing is syncing. Nobody needs the fade of the "Updated" text.
- **Fix:** remove the spinner and the fade. Do the blinking dot with a CSS `@keyframes` animation on `opacity`, so the browser handles it without your script. Add `prefers-reduced-motion` so it can be switched off.
- **Impact:** reduces E on the client for as long as the page is open. In Green Metrics Tool browser benchmarks (20-second animation), a `requestAnimationFrame` loop with style updates used about 161 J CPU energy and CSS `@keyframes` about 145 J, roughly 10% less. Removing an animation fully saves more than moving it to CSS.

#### [Medium] jQuery and Google Fonts for a simple table
- **Where:** the `<link>` to fonts.googleapis.com and the jQuery `<script>`
- **Issue:** two third-party requests plus font files, only to fill a table and set a font. jQuery is used here for `$.each`, `.html`, `.css`, `.text` and fades, and plain JavaScript does all of it. If the monitor PC has no internet, the page also loads without styles or without the table.
- **Fix:** use plain DOM code and a system font stack. The page then works offline and has no requests at all.
- **Impact:** reduces network transfer and parsing on every open of the report.

