**Workload:** web frontend (e-commerce category page, every visit)

#### [Security] Product data goes into `innerHTML` unescaped
- **Where:** the grid loop (`p.name`, `p.price`, `p.img`)
- **Issue:** if a name or image URL contains HTML, it runs as markup on your page. This is an XSS risk.
- **Fix:** escape the values (see the helper in the fix below), or build the nodes with `textContent`.

#### [High] All 2500 products are rendered at once
- **Where:** the `for` loop that fills `#grid`
- **Issue:** the page builds about 2500 cards with 5 wrapper `div`s each. That is more than 12,000 extra nodes for the browser to style, lay out and keep in memory. Most of them are far below the screen. Phones suffer most.
- **Fix:** render a first page and add more on request. Ideally the API also returns only one page at a time, so the rest is never sent. Also use one `article` per card instead of three nested wrappers.
- **Impact:** reduces client E and memory on every visit. It also cuts transferred bytes if the server paginates.

```javascript
const PAGE = 48;
let shown = 0;
const grid = document.getElementById("grid");
const moreBtn = document.getElementById("show-more");

const esc = (s) => String(s).replace(/[&<>"']/g, (c) =>
  ({ "&": "&amp;", "<": "&lt;", ">": "&gt;", '"': "&quot;", "'": "&#39;" })[c]);

function showMore() {
  const html = products.slice(shown, shown + PAGE).map((p) =>
    `<article class="card">
       <img src="${esc(p.img)}" alt="${esc(p.name)}">
       <span class="name">${esc(p.name)}</span>
       <span class="price">${esc(p.price)}</span>
     </article>`).join("");
  grid.insertAdjacentHTML("beforeend", html);
  shown = Math.min(shown + PAGE, products.length);
  moreBtn.hidden = shown >= products.length;
}

moreBtn.addEventListener("click", showMore);
showMore();
```

```html
<div id="grid"></div>
<button id="show-more" type="button">Show more products</button>
```

#### [High] `grid.innerHTML +=` inside the loop
- **Where:** the grid loop
- **Issue:** every `+=` serializes the whole grid to a string, appends, and parses it again. All earlier cards are destroyed and rebuilt each time, so the work grows quadratically with the product count. Image elements are also recreated.
- **Fix:** build one string and insert it once, as in `showMore()` above.

#### [High] Promo text is moved by a timer that never stops
- **Where:** `setInterval(..., 16)` on `#promo-text`
- **Issue:** it runs about 60 times per second for as long as the tab is open, even when the tab is in the background. Each tick writes `marginLeft`, which forces a layout. Then the next tick reads `offsetWidth`, which forces layout again. With a huge DOM, every layout is expensive, and this is likely why the fan is loud.
- **Fix:** use a CSS animation on `transform`. The browser can run it on the compositor without layout and without JavaScript.
- **Impact:** reduces client E for the whole time the page is open. In Green Metrics Tool measurements, a CSS `@keyframes` animation used about 10% less CPU energy than a JavaScript `requestAnimationFrame` version. Your 16 ms timer with forced layout is worse than that test case.

```css
.promo-bar { overflow: hidden; white-space: nowrap; }
#promo-text {
  display: inline-block;
  padding-left: 100%;
  animation: promo-scroll 20s linear infinite;
}
.promo-bar:hover #promo-text { animation-play-state: paused; }
@keyframes promo-scroll {
  from { transform: translateX(0); }
  to   { transform: translateX(-100%); }
}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  #promo-text { animation: none; padding-left: 0; }
}
```

Then delete the `setInterval` block. The `var promo` and `var x` lines are not needed any more.

#### [Low] Ship the text without motion if it is short
- **Where:** the promo bar
- **Issue:** three short messages may fit on a phone screen without scrolling. A static bar costs nothing.
- **Fix:** if you keep the scroll, the `prefers-reduced-motion` rule above already stops it for users who ask for less motion.

**Already good:** the data is read from one array, so no extra requests are made per product.

Landing page for our B2B analytics product, roughly 300k visits a month, people often leave the tab open while they read. The hero mockup floats up and down. How much energy would we save moving it to CSS? Just give me the diff, no essay.

```html
<section class="hero">
  <div class="hero-copy">
    <h1>See every pipeline in one place</h1>
    <p>Dashboards your whole team actually opens.</p>
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/demo">Book a demo</a>
  </div>
  <div class="hero-visual">
    <img id="mockup" src="/img/dashboard-mockup.svg" width="640" height="420" alt="Dashboard preview">
  </div>
</section>

<script>
  const mockup = document.getElementById('mockup');
  const start = performance.now();

  function float(now) {
    const t = (now - start) / 1000;
    const y = Math.sin(t * 1.2) * 12;
    const r = Math.sin(t * 0.6) * 1.5;
    mockup.style.transform = `translateY(${y}px) rotate(${r}deg)`;
    requestAnimationFrame(float);
  }
  requestAnimationFrame(float);
</script>
```

Diff: delete the `<script>` block and add this CSS.

```html
<style>
  #mockup {
    animation: float 5s ease-in-out infinite;
  }
  @keyframes float {
    0%, 100% { transform: translateY(-12px) rotate(-1.5deg); }
    50%      { transform: translateY(12px) rotate(1.5deg); }
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    #mockup { animation: none; }
  }
</style>
```

**Green Notes**
- **CSS keyframes instead of a JS animation loop** (reduces E): the browser runs a `transform` animation itself. Your script wakes up on every frame to compute `Math.sin` and write an inline style.
- **Measured reference:** in Green Metrics Tool browser benchmarks, a 20-second animation used ~161 J of CPU energy with `requestAnimationFrame` style updates and ~145 J with CSS `@keyframes`, about 10% less. That was a different animation on a different page. I can't scale it to 300k visits, because the real saving depends on dwell time, the device and how many frames are painted.
- **How to measure yours:** build both versions of the page. Run each as a Green Metrics Tool browser scenario that loads the page and idles for a fixed time, such as 60 seconds. Compare the CPU energy of the two runs. Then weight the per-second difference by your real average time on page. Your analytics should have that number.
- **Behavior change:** the two sine waves had different periods, so tilt and bob drifted apart. In the CSS version they are locked together in one 5 s loop. I also added `prefers-reduced-motion`, which the script did not respect.

The saving is modest. If the motion is not needed, removing it saves more than any rewrite.

