```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>API request log</title>
  <style>
    body { font-family: sans-serif; margin: 16px; }
    #live-dot {
      display: inline-block; width: 10px; height: 10px; border-radius: 50%; background: #2a9d4b;
      animation: pulse 1.2s ease-in-out infinite alternate;
    }
    @keyframes pulse { from { opacity: 1; } to { opacity: 0.2; } }
    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { #live-dot { animation: none; } }
    table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; }
    td, th { border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; padding: 4px 8px; text-align: left; }
    tr.s5 td:nth-child(3) { color: #c0392b; font-weight: bold; }
    #pager { margin: 8px 0; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <h1>API requests <span id="live-dot"></span> Live</h1>
  <select id="status-filter">
    <option value="all">All</option>
    <option value="2">2xx</option>
    <option value="4">4xx</option>
    <option value="5">5xx</option>
  </select>
  <div id="pager">
    <button id="prev" type="button">Prev</button>
    <span id="page-info"></span>
    <button id="next" type="button">Next</button>
  </div>
  <table id="log">
    <thead><tr><th>Time</th><th>Path</th><th>Status</th><th>ms</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody id="rows"></tbody>
  </table>

  <script>
    const PAGE_SIZE = 100;
    let logs = [];
    let page = 0;

    const rowsEl = document.getElementById('rows');
    const filterEl = document.getElementById('status-filter');

    async function load() {
      const res = await fetch('/api/admin/requests?limit=2000');
      logs = await res.json();
      render();
    }

    function render() {
      const f = filterEl.value;
      const visible = f === 'all' ? logs : logs.filter(l => String(l.status).startsWith(f));
      const pages = Math.max(1, Math.ceil(visible.length / PAGE_SIZE));
      page = Math.min(page, pages - 1);

      const frag = document.createDocumentFragment();
      for (const l of visible.slice(page * PAGE_SIZE, (page + 1) * PAGE_SIZE)) {
        const tr = document.createElement('tr');
        tr.className = 's' + String(l.status)[0];
        for (const value of [l.time, l.path, l.status, l.ms]) {
          const td = document.createElement('td');
          td.textContent = value;
          tr.appendChild(td);
        }
        frag.appendChild(tr);
      }
      rowsEl.replaceChildren(frag);

      document.getElementById('page-info').textContent =
        'Page ' + (page + 1) + ' of ' + pages + ' (' + visible.length + ' requests)';
      document.getElementById('prev').disabled = page === 0;
      document.getElementById('next').disabled = page >= pages - 1;
    }

    filterEl.addEventListener('change', () => { page = 0; render(); });
    document.getElementById('prev').addEventListener('click', () => { page--; render(); });
    document.getElementById('next').addEventListener('click', () => { page++; render(); });

    setInterval(load, 10000);
    load();
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

**Green Notes**
- **CSS keyframes instead of a 50 ms JS timer** (reduces E): the old timer woke the main thread 20 times per second, all day, just to change one opacity value. Now the browser animates `opacity` itself and no script runs. In Green Metrics Tool browser benchmarks, a 20-second animation used about 161 J CPU energy with `requestAnimationFrame` style updates and about 145 J with CSS `@keyframes` (about 10% less). The gap is small per second but this page runs for hours. I also added `prefers-reduced-motion` so the dot stops pulsing for users who turn animations off.
- **100 rows in the DOM instead of up to 2000** (reduces E): the old page rebuilt a table of up to 2000 rows every 10 seconds, and the browser had to parse, style and lay it out each time. Now only one page of rows exists. The filter works on the full list, so you still see all matching requests, one page at a time.
- **Behavior changes:** you now page through the results (Prev/Next), and changing the filter goes back to page 1. A refresh keeps your current page, unless it no longer exists.
- **Bug fixed:** the old code put `l.path` straight into `innerHTML`. A request path with markup in it would be run as HTML. `textContent` shows it as plain text.
- *Not applied:* a smaller `limit` in the API call. That needs a backend change, and it would cut transferred data further. It is worth doing later.

```nginx
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name app.example.com;
    root /srv/app/dist;

    # serve the .gz files created at build time, fall back to compressing on the fly
    gzip_static on;
    gzip on;
    gzip_comp_level 5;
    gzip_min_length 1024;
    gzip_types text/css application/javascript application/json image/svg+xml;
    gzip_vary on;

    location /assets/ {
        expires 1y;
        add_header Cache-Control "public, immutable";
        try_files $uri =404;
    }

    location / {
        add_header Cache-Control "no-cache";
        try_files $uri /index.html;
    }
}
```

Create the `.gz` files in the build, next to the originals:

```bash
find /srv/app/dist -type f \( -name '*.js' -o -name '*.css' -o -name '*.json' -o -name '*.html' -o -name '*.svg' \) \
    -exec gzip -k -9 {} +
```

**Green Notes**
- **Precompressed assets** (reduces E, network): each file is compressed once at build time instead of on every request. In Green Metrics Tool measurements of nginx serving text assets, compression sent about 10× fewer bytes, and compressing on every request cost about 4× the CPU energy of serving precompressed files. Level 9 is fine here because it runs once per build.
- **Long caching for hashed assets** (reduces network): `immutable` lets browsers skip revalidation, while `index.html` is always revalidated so new deploys show up.

